WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:05.440
Squid Game has been an absolute breakout success&nbsp;
and that’s bad news for Disney. The show is doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:10.640
so well that even the head of Netflix seemed&nbsp;
surprised by it. There’s a show on Netflix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:13.280
right now that is the number one in the world.&nbsp;
Everywhere in the world. It’s called Squid Game.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:18.160
Squid Game will definitely be our biggest&nbsp;
non-english show in the world for sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:22.640
it’s only been out for 9 days. And it’s a very&nbsp;
good chance that it’s going to be our biggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:27.840
show ever. But there’s actually a few really great&nbsp;
reasons why Netflix’s strategy here paid off so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:32.880
huge. They are basically doing the exact opposite&nbsp;
of Disney when it comes to creating new shows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:37.200
and people are starting to pay attention. Even&nbsp;
Jeff Bezos had to pay his respects on Twitter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:42.000
to how brilliant this strategy was, calling&nbsp;
it impressive and inspiring. Now, naturally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.200
people were skeptical that he would be&nbsp;
paying Netflix such a big compliment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:50.640
since he did create Amazon Prime Video after all,&nbsp;
but Bezos responded to those comments directly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:56.400
saying that No, it was just simple admiration.&nbsp;
So why is Bezos so excited about Squid Game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:01.440
even though he hadn’t seen the show yet? Well,&nbsp;
Bezos is a businessman first and foremost,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:05.440
and he understands that Squid Game is going&nbsp;
to change everything for the world of online&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:09.680
streaming. And the business strategy here is&nbsp;
interesting, but I think the technological&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:14.400
implications here might be even bigger, since&nbsp;
Squid Game could mark the key turning point for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:19.120
the commercial use of deep fakes, but we’ll get&nbsp;
to that in a bit. First, we need to cover why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:23.760
Squid Game is so successful in the first place.&nbsp;
This isn’t the first time a foreign language show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:28.480
has broken out and gone viral globally. Narcos&nbsp;
captured everyone’s attention in 2015 with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:33.280
wild story of Pablo Escobar, and the German&nbsp;
time-travel series Dark was wildly successful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:38.400
when it launched in 2017. But Squid Game is a&nbsp;
little different, mostly because of the speed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:43.280
with which it has gained popularity. Just two&nbsp;
weeks after launch, Squid Game became a massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:48.640
phenomenon and topped the charts in 90 different&nbsp;
countries, including the United States. The core&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:53.920
reason that Squid Game is so universally popular&nbsp;
and hard to put down is that the subject matter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:58.800
is incredibly relatable. The main plot centers&nbsp;
around a game show with deadly consequences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:03.520
it’s essentially a last man standing competition&nbsp;
where whoever can stay alive the longest wins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:08.480
millions of dollars. Everyone has a relationship&nbsp;
with money and everyone fears death at some level.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:13.680
These themes cut across cultures and allow anyone&nbsp;
with a pulse to clearly understand the stakes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:17.600
and get drawn into the story. But there’s&nbsp;
another reason why I think Squid Game has been so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:22.800
successful. The subplot. See, while the main plot&nbsp;
centers around a group of characters fighting for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:28.320
their lives, and millions of dollars, the show&nbsp;
introduces a detective character who is trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:33.600
to uncover what’s really going on. Mysteries&nbsp;
also make for incredibly engaging stories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:38.560
so Squid Game is basically putting one great&nbsp;
story within another great story. If you were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:43.520
to delete all the scenes where the contestants&nbsp;
are playing the actual games, and just watch the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:48.160
detective work the case, I think you’d still be&nbsp;
very entertained. By combining these two addictive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:52.560
genres, the creators of the show are basically&nbsp;
guaranteeing that you won't be able to put it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:57.680
down. And this strategy works especially well&nbsp;
because Netflix releases all the episodes at once.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:02.960
Binge watching is a critical part of the Netflix&nbsp;
content strategy, first and foremost because it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:08.880
just what viewers want. But there is a major&nbsp;
drawback. By pumping out content all at once,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:13.760
it’s much harder for Netflix to drive attention&nbsp;
through positive reviews from critics. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:19.360
Netflix didn’t even mail out screeners to the&nbsp;
press before the show went live, which is very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:23.520
abnormal. This could be because no one, not&nbsp;
even Netflix, could have predicted exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:28.480
how successful Squid Game was going to be, but&nbsp;
it’s also part of their forward looking strategy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:34.080
Reviews from established critics are increasingly&nbsp;
irrelevant, just look at the consistent disparity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:38.880
between Dave Chappelle’s critic scores and&nbsp;
audience scores on Rotten Tomatoes. And Netflix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:43.440
has the power to design around bad critic scores.&nbsp;
The Netflix app could easily integrate with Rotten&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:48.480
Tomatoes or Metacritic to show users an aggregated&nbsp;
rating before recommending a movie or show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:53.920
but they don’t. This is because Netflix is driven&nbsp;
by an algorithm and only cares about finding a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:58.880
show that you, the current viewer, will like.&nbsp;
Clearly lots of people disagree with critics and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:03.920
love shows that get bad reviews, and Netflix is&nbsp;
happy to serve that content to the people who will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:10.240
enjoy it. When Netflix releases 9 hours of content&nbsp;
in one go, reviewers often can’t even watch it all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:14.800
to write a review before the show has gone viral.&nbsp;
And that’s a big advantage of the bingable release&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:20.080
strategy. The flip side of all this is the Disney&nbsp;
strategy. Two years ago, they launched Disney+&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:24.960
and the streaming service was an overwhelming&nbsp;
success. The service broke 100 million users&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:30.400
ahead of schedule and now Disney is doubling-down&nbsp;
and aiming to release one new piece of content&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:35.360
every week. There’s a problem though. Even though&nbsp;
Disney’s content is, on average, extremely high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:41.280
quality, they don’t have very much of it… this&nbsp;
chart shows the ratio of good shows to bad shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:47.120
for every major streaming service, and Disney&nbsp;
ranks incredibly well, especially for movies. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:52.720
when you look at the total size of the Disney plus&nbsp;
content library, it’s the smallest of any service.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:57.920
This makes sense though, Disney spends billions of&nbsp;
dollars developing major franchises like Marvel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:02.640
Star Wars, and the Disney princesses. They simply&nbsp;
aren’t in the business of taking lots of risks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:06.960
on smaller projects, they would much rather&nbsp;
spend billions acquiring a company like Marvel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:11.840
that everyone already knows about. This leads them&nbsp;
to miss out on wildcards like Squid Game though.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:16.480
The most impressive thing about all this Squid&nbsp;
Game virality is that it was based on no existing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:21.200
intellectual property. It wasn’t adapted from a&nbsp;
book or video game, and it still succeeded with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:26.080
no built-in fan base. Now, you could say that&nbsp;
it builds upon established works in the broader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:31.200
battle royale genre, especially movies like As the&nbsp;
Gods Will, but there’s nothing stopping Disney,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:36.640
Amazon Prime, or Apple from developing a similar&nbsp;
title. They haven’t though, and the reason comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:41.840
back to the Netflix core strategy. Netflix&nbsp;
focuses relentlessly on user engagement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:46.080
The team there understands that every night,&nbsp;
they are competing with every other app to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:50.800
deliver something interesting to watch. That means&nbsp;
having lots of content. Instead of putting massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:56.080
resources into a single franchise, which people&nbsp;
could get tired of, Netflix focuses on taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.080 --> 00:06:02.080
risks on lots of new projects. Many of them fail,&nbsp;
but when one breaks out, it changes everything.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:06.880
Stock in Netflix hit all time highs shortly after&nbsp;
Squid Game went viral, so the strategy seems to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:11.440
be working. There is another Netflix competitor&nbsp;
that we haven’t talked about yet though, and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:16.320
have way more content than any other platform by&nbsp;
far. I’m talking about YouTube of course, which,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:21.600
in some ways, is the Netflix model on steroids.&nbsp;
There’s now 500 hours of content uploaded to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:27.120
YouTube every minute, which, now that I’m saying&nbsp;
it out loud, makes me think it’s kind of crazy to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:32.640
compete with all that. But anyway, great content&nbsp;
clearly rises to the top on YouTube, just like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:37.680
Squid Game did on Netflix, and increasingly,&nbsp;
in order to win in the content streaming game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:42.560
you just need the most content. This all bodes&nbsp;
well for Netflix, but we still have to discuss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:47.520
internationalization and the future of deep fakes.&nbsp;
Netflix is by far the most aggressive streaming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:53.520
service when it comes to translation. They now&nbsp;
offer subtitles in 37 different languages and dubs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:58.640
in 34 different languages. This helps increase&nbsp;
the reach of every title in the Netflix library,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.640 --> 00:07:03.680
and no, I’m not about to transition into a&nbsp;
VPN ad. Translation and dubbing are one thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:08.320
but they don’t always work out as planned. I’ve&nbsp;
seen tons of videos floating around about how bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:12.960
the English dubs were for Squid Game and yeah,&nbsp;
they’re pretty bad. But technology is about to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:17.360
change all of that. Deep fakes are already pretty&nbsp;
impressive on their own, but they don’t have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:22.240
ton of practical uses. Sure, you can make a funny&nbsp;
video, but they aren’t widely used in Hollywood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:26.720
at least not yet. Part of the problem with deep&nbsp;
fakes is that, in order to get a really realistic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:31.520
result, you need an actor to stand in and give a&nbsp;
convincing performance. They will definitely help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:36.400
movie studios bring back actors to reprise famous&nbsp;
roles long after they are gone, but that’s a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:41.600
of a niche use case. Deep Fakes will also probably&nbsp;
help for future projects like the Irishman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:46.640
where older actors are digitally de-aged to&nbsp;
play their younger selves within a story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:52.320
but I still think that won’t be as impactful as&nbsp;
what deep fakes could do for internationalization.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:57.200
Two years ago, a team of machine learning&nbsp;
researchers released a paper called “Text-based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.200 --> 00:08:02.080
Editing of Talking-head Video.” In it, they&nbsp;
describe a method for automatically generating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:06.560
mouth movements to match words that were never&nbsp;
spoken by the actor. Take a look at this sample:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:12.560
I love the smell of napalm in the morning, I&nbsp;
love the smell of french toast in the morning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:17.360
They also used this method to synthesize&nbsp;
video of that guy speaking German. Here we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:23.520
enable our non-German-speaking subject to speak&nbsp;
German. Ich spreche Deutsch. Even though this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:28.400
just a two-year-old demo, the results are pretty&nbsp;
remarkable. I really don’t think it will be long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:33.200
until this technology is rolled out at companies&nbsp;
like Netflix across the entire content catalogue.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:38.400
There are still a few major hurdles, like dealing&nbsp;
with odd lighting scenarios and shots with heavy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:43.120
visual effects, but it definitely seems within&nbsp;
reach. Obviously, you’ll still need a great voice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:48.240
actor to bring the performance to life, but AI&nbsp;
is getting better at generating realistic voices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:53.680
as well, so it’s entirely possible that in a few&nbsp;
years, translation, dubbing, and mouth remapping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:58.000
will be fully automated. Deep fakes are endlessly&nbsp;
fascinating to me, so if you’re working on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:02.080
anything related to this, I’d love to get in touch&nbsp;
with you to talk more about it. In the meantime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:07.840
please check out this recommended video, the&nbsp;
YouTube algorithm thinks you’ll really like it.

